From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 05:46:14 +0000
Subject: build: declare the files esbuild reads and writes in bundle steps

The esbuild bundle steps read and write files that are not declared in
GN, so they only work when run in the source tree, and they can't run on
a remote worker (e.g. Siso with remote execution), where only declared
inputs are present and only declared outputs come back:

* esbuild reads the package.json and tsconfig.json files (and the
  tsconfig.json "extends" chain) above each file it bundles. Without
  front_end/tsconfig.json and config/typescript/tsconfig.base.json
  ("strict": true), iife bundles lose their "use strict" prefix. The
  package.json files of third_party packages set "type" and
  "sideEffects", and csp_evaluator's tsconfig.json sets its own target.
* source-map-scopes-codec's .ts sources import "./codec.js" etc. esbuild
  bundles the prebuilt package/src/*.js files next to them, which are not
  declared; without them it falls back to the .ts files and produces a
  different bundle.
* With --sourcemap esbuild follows the sourceMappingURL of bundled
  third_party .js files, reads their .js.map files and, when a map has no
  sourcesContent (puppeteer, wasmparser), the original sources it points
  at. Without them the output .js.map points at the .js files instead.
* --sourcemap writes <outfile>.map, which is not a declared output.

Declare them: the repo-wide config files in the bundle template, the
<outfile>.map output when sourcemaps are on, and the per-package files as
inputs of each third_party package target, which bundles inherit through
the package's _sources group. inspector_overlay declares its own
tsconfig.json.

This should be upstreamed.

